cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use spectraMR in your research, please cite the following."
title: "spectraMR: A Multi-Paradigm Research Framework for MRI Reconstruction, Super-Resolution, and Generative Modelling"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Adnane
    family-names: Gdihi
    email: adnane.gdihi@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6266-7769"
# `version` tracks src/spectramr/__init__.py, which hatch reads to build the wheel.
# `date-released` is stamped when the tag is actually cut: it asserted 2026-08-01
# while no tag, GitHub release or PyPI distribution existed, and a citation is the
# one file whose whole job is to be verifiable.
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-04"
license: Apache-2.0
# `doi` is the CONCEPT DOI -- the parent record that always redirects to the newest
# version. The v0.1.0 deposit's own (version) DOI is listed under `identifiers`. Getting
# these the wrong way round is the failure this ordering exists to prevent: a citation
# pinned to the version DOI keeps sending readers to v0.1.0 forever. The number has one
# owner, `scripts/release/zenodo_deposit.CONCEPT_DOI`, and a test pins this file to it.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22291316"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22291317"
    description: "Archived snapshot of the v0.1.0 release."
repository-code: "https://github.com/adnaneGdihi/spectramr"
url: "https://github.com/adnaneGdihi/spectramr"
keywords:
  - magnetic-resonance-imaging
  - image-reconstruction
  - generative-models
  - diffusion-models
  - physics-informed-neural-networks
  - quantitative-mri
  - deep-learning
